WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.760
Speaker 1: Welcome to the discipleship experience.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: What a time we've been having.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:14.240
Speaker 1: In the last class, we talked about the importance of evangelizing, and today we're going to be learning how to evangelize Muslims.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:22.560
Speaker 1: I have with me pastor Tomiwa Emanuel from cciebajo and pastor David Inuoluwa from CCIE Corodo.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.750
Speaker 1: It is important to note that we have people around us.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:34.150
Speaker 1: Our colleagues, friends, family members, maybe, or people who meet online who are Muslims.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.230
Speaker 1: And we have to be able to make meaningful conversations.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.190
Speaker 1: To have meaningful conversations, please, about the gospel.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:43.750
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:01:00.590
Speaker 1: I've come to realize that before you can teach about something or defend or preach the gospel or talk about Jesus, you have to understand the worldview of the people you're talking to.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:06.590
Speaker 1: You have to understand it, because that's the only way you can present a breakthrough their hearts.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.750
Speaker 1: So I would like to ask this question.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.070
Speaker 1: How did Islam come to me?

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.470
Speaker 3: All right, thank you, Pastor Ituno.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:25.160
Speaker 3: Very, very powerful way to end that, especially the part where you said, we have to get their worldview, we have to know what they believe, we have to know what they think, and we have to understand it before we can engage.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.000
Speaker 3: The religion of Islam began when Prophet Muhammad had his encounter in a cave.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:44.040
Speaker 3: Now, Prophet Muhammad was born about 600 years after the death of Jesus Christ, and he grew up as a man, married his first wife, I think she was 40, he was 25.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.350
Speaker 3: And then they left Medina and moved to Mecca.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:58.310
Speaker 3: And then he around, there was a period where he would withdraw himself, you know, go away solitary times to reflect and to worship.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:02.430
Speaker 3: And then in one of those times where he went was in a cave called the cave of Hira.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.950
Speaker 3: And he had an encounter with a spirit being.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.150
Speaker 3: And the encounter, as described in Islamic sources, was a very unpleasant one.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:22.670
Speaker 3: And that's me being gentle with descriptions, because according to Islamic sources, the spirit being pressed on him violently and forcibly three times, saying, here's this book read.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.910
Speaker 3: And he would say, I don't know how to read.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.070
Speaker 3: And then the spirit being would press on him again.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:32.990
Speaker 3: And the encounter was so bad, at the end of the day, he came out of it, you know, very fearful.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.510
Speaker 3: He was traumatized.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:38.190
Speaker 3: He needed his wife to cover him up, you know, and all of that.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:48.270
Speaker 3: And then his wife had an elderly relative called Warakat who was, according to Islamic sources, a Christian, or at least had some knowledge of Christian traditions.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:03:02.590
Speaker 3: And Warakat said, oh, this being that you encountered must have been the same spirit that was sent to Moses from Allah and hence the beginning of the journey of Revelations as claimed by Muhammad.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:08.660
Speaker 3: And these revelations were compiled by his scribes and his companions into what we have today as the Quran.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.860
Speaker 3: And that was the beginning of Islam.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:25.780
Speaker 3: Out of this whole period of revelations came the central theme of Islam which is that there is no other God but Allah and Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.300
Speaker 3: This central theme is what holds every Muslim together.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.340
Speaker 3: So there is no Muslim that does not know this.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.670
Speaker 3: It is the very core of Islamic belief.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.950
Speaker 3: There is no God but Allah and Muhammad his messenger.

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:46.150
Speaker 3: And that, my friends, is the brief summarized history and beginning of Islam.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.230
Speaker 1: Thank you Pastor Mo for that.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:55.790
Speaker 1: I believe there are other things that Muslims practice beyond beliefs.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.720
Speaker 1: What practices ship the everyday life of a Muslim.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:10.640
Speaker 2: So as Pastor Tomua said, the first thing a Muslim must acknowledge is that there is no God but Allah and Muhammad is his messenger.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.400
Speaker 2: They call that the shahada.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.560
Speaker 2: So that's like the foundation of everything.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.160
Speaker 2: The second is Salat, which is they have to pray a certain amount of times.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.760
Speaker 2: Facing Mecca, specifically five times a day they have to pray.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.400
Speaker 2: The third is Swan S W A N which is the fasting.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.600
Speaker 2: So they fast during a particular period in a year.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:43.520
Speaker 2: The fourth is Zakat where they have to give a certain amount of money to Mecca.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:44.240
Speaker 2: Right.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.480
Speaker 2: And the fifth is A.J.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:53.040
Speaker 2: It's like a P journey where they have to go to Mecca and perform some rights and all that stuff.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.940
Speaker 2: So these are like the five major practices of what it means to be a Muslim.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:05.940
Speaker 1: So listening to all that you've said, it's obvious that Islam is not just a religion that you practice on the weekend.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:11.620
Speaker 1: I've heard a lot of people say, oh, Christianity and Islam are the same.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:14.980
Speaker 1: We serve the same God.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.540
Speaker 1: There are so many similarities in the Bible to the Quran.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:22.100
Speaker 1: Similar characters, similar characters.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.070
Speaker 1: You hear people say, oh, Jesus is actually acknowledged in Islam.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:27.910
Speaker 1: We honor Jesus.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:28.830
Speaker 1: He's a prophet.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.990
Speaker 1: Are these claims true?

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.630
Speaker 3: They are fundamentally false.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:37.710
Speaker 3: And it's important that we understand that this is not an inflammatory statement.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.590
Speaker 3: It is not a statement that is made out of, you know, desire to cause problem.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.510
Speaker 3: It is just the facts.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:55.270
Speaker 3: It is true that there are similarities, but it does not make them the same because we have to look at what these religions hold to.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.430
Speaker 3: These religions are not built on opinions.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.430
Speaker 3: They're not built on feelings or emotions.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:06.230
Speaker 3: They are built on texts and scriptures that call themselves the pillars of truth.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:12.190
Speaker 3: All right, and so let me start by talking about the idea of God in both religions.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.470
Speaker 3: Because there's this idea that, oh, it's God.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.030
Speaker 3: We say God or the Muslims say Allah, we say Jesus.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:24.770
Speaker 3: The Muslims say Isa, we say David, they say Dawda, we say Moses, say Musa, we say Abraham, they say Ibrahim.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:26.010
Speaker 3: So it's just a language thing.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:26.770
Speaker 3: That's not true.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:27.730
Speaker 3: And here's why.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:36.610
Speaker 3: The entire concept, once you get past the problem of atheism and you acknowledge that there is a God, we understand that this God is transcendent.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.130
Speaker 3: He transcends time, space and matter.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:43.570
Speaker 3: The only way we can find him is if he finds us, comes to tell us who he is.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:48.610
Speaker 3: So we cannot know God aside from how God introduces himself.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:55.780
Speaker 3: So how does God introduce himself in Christianity and how does God introduce himself in Islam?

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.060
Speaker 3: That's how we can know if we are serving the same God.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:09.940
Speaker 3: Because if I say that I am a teacher who flogs, and Pastor Ini says he's a teacher who only puts students, you know, on punishment, he doesn't flog.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:16.100
Speaker 3: A student can't come and say both of us are the same teacher because I have introduced myself as a teacher who flogs.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.050
Speaker 3: That fundamentally separates us.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.370
Speaker 3: He rejects flogging.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:20.130
Speaker 3: I flog.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:21.250
Speaker 3: I don't vlog.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:28.490
Speaker 3: But the God of the Bible introduces himself as triune in nature.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:29.250
